  "AI content payment is not merely a copyright or transaction-efficiency problem; it is a question of political-economic legitimacy. As AI valuations reach hundreds of billions, society asks why the gains from public web content and human knowledge accrue to a few firms. The paper proposes a six-layer tiered data market — from free public content through scarce verified datasets to public benefit-sharing — and defines the AI network democratic economy: rights, pricing, participation, auditability, and returns, each democratized through machine-readable protocols like AICR/AICL.": "IA kunnafoni sara-ko tɛ sɛbɛnnitigiya walima jago-teliya gɛlɛya dɔrɔn ye; a ye fanga-nafolo bɛnni-ko ye. IA nafolo latigɛli minnu bɛ se milyari kɛmɛw ma, jamakulu bɛ ɲininka mun na nafa minnu bɛ bɔ ɛntɛrinɛti kunnafoni jama ta ani hadamaden lɔnniya la, olu bɛ don sosiyete damadɔ dɔrɔn bolo. Sɛbɛn in bɛ kunnafoni-jago jɔyɔrɔ wɔɔrɔ fɔ, minnu bɛ jɔyɔrɔ caman na — ka bɔ kunnafoni jama-ta fu na ka taa kunnafoni-jɛlen sɛgɛsɛgɛlen dɔgɔmanw na fo jama ka nafa tilali — wa a bɛ IA jɛnkulu nafolo-ko demokarasiman jira: josiraw, sɔngɔ-latigɛli, tonbaaraw sɔrɔli, sɛgɛsɛgɛli sebagaya, ani seginnifɛnw, o bɛɛ demokarasiya masini-kalanni protokolo fɛ, i n'a fɔ AICR/AICL.",
